  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/f17e4718742158f65db70bd25e0ba97fd0fba793b5212eee0ee60072eb4231e9.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+16+{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x+8 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+2 & =0 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-2$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\ny=x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=x$ intersects the circle $(x+4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=8$ at the point $(-2,-2).$",
